## Phase 1: Coordinate-space and geometry model

Goal: implement and exhaustively unit-test deterministic conversion without
changing public command behavior.

### Tasks

✔ Add `src/dwarfspec/pointer_spaces.lua` using
  `dwarfspec.immutable_enum`.
✔ Add the enum to the production namespace and reserved-command schema.
✔ Add LuaLS declarations for `dwarfspec.EPointerSpace` and
  `DS.EPointerSpace`.
✔ Add an injected effective-geometry provider with the production `gps`
  implementation.
✔ Add documented adapter helpers for geometry validation and paired-position
  normalization.
✔ Add unit coverage for grid-to-center-pixel conversion with non-square cell
  sizes.
✔ Add unit coverage for exact pixel preservation and pixel-to-grid conversion.
✔ Add unit coverage for right and bottom residual pixels clamping to the last
  grid cell.
✔ Add unit coverage for every invalid coordinate and invalid geometry field.
✔ Add immutable-enum and reserved-name coverage.

### Phase 1 exit gate

✔ Conversion has one production implementation.
✔ Conversion tests do not access real DF globals.
✔ The enum is immutable and exported but no command accepts it yet.
✔ Focused and full unit suites pass.
✔ Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

### Phase 1 implementation notes

- `pointer_adapter.normalize_position()` is the sole conversion boundary. Grid
  input maps to cell-center pixels; pixel input is preserved exactly while its
  grid coordinate is derived and clamped for residual right and bottom pixels.
- The adapter validates and defensively copies all six effective-geometry
  fields. Errors identify the corresponding `gps` field.
- `ds.lua` injects a provider that reads current values from `df.global.gps`.
  Conversion tests supply local geometry tables and do not read DF globals.
- `ds.EPointerSpace` exports immutable `GRID` and `PIXELS` values and is
  reserved against extension-command conflicts. No pointer command parses a
  coordinate-space argument in this phase.
- The rockspec discovers source modules automatically, so the new enum requires
  no explicit package-manifest entry.

