## Phase 3: Public command migration

Goal: route all public pointer and mouse commands through paired ownership.

### Tasks

✔ Extend `ds.move_pointer()` overload parsing with the optional pointer space.
✔ Preserve the existing numeric grid overload and exact errors where their
  contract has not changed.
✔ Route numeric grid input through fresh geometry and paired normalization.
✔ Route numeric pixel input through fresh geometry and paired normalization.
✔ Keep subject bounds clipping and anchor selection in grid coordinates, then
  normalize the selected cell into paired state.
✔ Extend `ds.hover()` numeric routing to accept the pointer space.
✔ Keep fluent subject calls implicitly grid-based.
✔ Update `ds.mouseInput()` to read the paired position and call `sync()` before
  dispatch.
✔ Update `ds.click()` to use the paired pointer produced by its subject move and
  call the common synchronization path before dispatch.
✔ Ensure button down/up state transitions resynchronize both coordinate pairs.
✔ Update command-level unit fixtures to model:
  - `getMousePixels`;
  - precise raw mouse fields;
  - effective grid and pixel geometry.
✔ Add command-level unit coverage for default grid, explicit grid, explicit
  pixels, subject anchors, hover, clicks, wheel input, button transitions,
  repeat moves, invalid space, and all cleanup paths.
✔ Update cleanup-state expectations without weakening existing screen,
  subject, button, or render assertions.

### Phase 3 exit gate

✔ Every public pointer-changing route reaches the same adapter owner.
✔ Existing grid-coordinate call sites remain source-compatible.
✔ Pixel calls preserve exact requested pixels and expose the derived grid.
✔ Subject calls still return their selected grid coordinates.
✔ Focused and full unit suites pass.
✔ Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

### Phase 3 implementation notes

- `ds.move_pointer(x, y)` remains the default grid overload with its prior
  validation messages. Explicit `GRID` follows the same path; explicit
  `PIXELS` validates against current pixel dimensions and returns the exact
  requested pixels.
- `EPointerSpace` uses immutable numeric enum values so raw `"grid"` and
  `"pixels"` strings are distinguishable and rejected. Callers use the stable
  `GRID` and `PIXELS` member names rather than their backing values.
- Numeric moves and hovers read fresh effective geometry, normalize once, and
  call the paired adapter owner. Residual right and bottom pixels retain their
  exact pixel coordinate while exposing the clamped final grid cell.
- Subject bounds and anchors remain grid-based. Top-level and fluent subject
  calls reject coordinate-space arguments instead of silently ignoring them.
- Mouse clicks, wheel input, subject clicks, button down, and button up all
  resynchronize both raw coordinate pairs immediately before dispatch.
- Cleanup tests now assert both accessors, both raw pairs, pointer-probe state,
  button state, subject cleanup, native attachment state, and render cleanup
  across explicit unmount, failure reset, abort/recovery, and repeat cycles.

